// Adversarial cross-backend PR review.
//
// Three reviewers on three different backends read the same diff through three
// different lenses. A skeptic on a fourth backend tries to REFUTE everything
// they report. The coordinator merges what survived, re-scatters the reviewers
// at whatever has not been examined yet, and stops when a round adds nothing
// new or the round cap is reached. Read-only: nothing is posted to GitHub.
team AdversarialPRReview[coordinator     : ClaudeCode,
                         rev_correctness : ClaudeCode,
                         rev_security    : Codex,
                         rev_quality     : Cursor,
                         skeptic         : OpenCode]
{
    input  pr         : int     // pull request number, read with `gh pr diff`
    input  max_rounds : int     // hard cap on scatter rounds, bounds the loop
    output report     : string  // the entire deliverable

    action round_brief : string // coordinator -> all three reviewers
    action correctness : string
    action security    : string
    action quality     : string
    action ledger      : string // skeptic -> coordinator, this round's verdicts

    // ---- seed the first round ------------------------------------------------
    prompt coordinator(pr, max_rounds) -> round_brief
    "
        You are coordinating an adversarial review of GitHub pull request #$(pr)
        in the current repository. The review runs for at most $(max_rounds) rounds.

        Run `gh pr view $(pr)` and `gh pr diff $(pr)` to see what is being changed.
        Do not modify any files and do not post anything to GitHub. This whole run
        is read-only.

        Set `round_brief` to a briefing for three parallel reviewers containing,
        in plain text:
          - the line `PR: $(pr)`
          - the line `ROUND: 1 of $(max_rounds)`
          - two sentences on what the PR is trying to do
          - the changed files worth attention, and which look riskiest
          - the line `CONFIRMED SO FAR: none`
          - the line `REFUTED SO FAR: none`

        Keep it under 300 words. The reviewers can run `gh pr diff` themselves, so
        do not paste the diff into the briefing.
    "

    // ---- three lenses, three backends, one tag -------------------------------
    prompt rev_correctness(round_brief) -> correctness
    "
        $(round_brief)

        Review this pull request for CORRECTNESS only: logic errors, unhandled edge
        cases, off-by-one and boundary mistakes, race conditions and ordering bugs,
        error paths that swallow failures, API contracts used wrongly, and tests
        that do not actually test what they claim. Read the diff with `gh pr diff`
        using the PR number in the briefing above, and open the surrounding files
        for context. Do not modify anything.

        Ignore anything already listed as CONFIRMED or REFUTED in the briefing, and
        prioritise wherever the briefing says nobody has looked yet.

        Set `correctness` to a numbered list. Every entry gives the file, the line
        or symbol, one sentence naming the defect, and one concrete failure
        scenario: the inputs or state that produce the wrong result. No style
        opinions, no praise, no summary. If you find nothing new, set `correctness`
        to exactly `NONE`.
    "

    prompt rev_security(round_brief) -> security
    "
        $(round_brief)

        Review this pull request for SECURITY and ROBUSTNESS only: injection and
        unescaped interpolation, path traversal, unvalidated external input, authn
        and authz gaps, secrets or tokens in code and logs, unsafe deserialisation,
        TOCTOU races, resource exhaustion and unbounded growth, panics or unwraps
        reachable from untrusted input, and CORS or network exposure that is wider
        than it needs to be. Read the diff with `gh pr diff` using the PR number in
        the briefing above, and open the surrounding files for context. Do not
        modify anything.

        Ignore anything already listed as CONFIRMED or REFUTED in the briefing, and
        prioritise wherever the briefing says nobody has looked yet.

        Set `security` to a numbered list. Every entry gives the file, the line or
        symbol, one sentence naming the weakness, and one concrete attack or
        failure path that reaches it. Say who has to be able to do what for it to
        matter. If you find nothing new, set `security` to exactly `NONE`.
    "

    prompt rev_quality(round_brief) -> quality
    "
        $(round_brief)

        Review this pull request for DESIGN QUALITY only: duplicated logic that
        should be shared, abstractions built for a single caller, dead or
        unreachable code the change leaves behind, functions doing several
        unrelated jobs, names that mislead, error handling for cases that cannot
        happen, and complexity that a simpler construction would remove. Read the
        diff with `gh pr diff` using the PR number in the briefing above, and open
        the surrounding files for context. Do not modify anything.

        Ignore anything already listed as CONFIRMED or REFUTED in the briefing, and
        prioritise wherever the briefing says nobody has looked yet.

        Set `quality` to a numbered list. Every entry gives the file, the line or
        symbol, one sentence naming the problem, and the concrete cost of leaving
        it: what breaks later, or what a reader gets wrong. Formatting nits and
        personal preference do not count. If you find nothing new, set `quality` to
        exactly `NONE`.
    "

    // ---- the tag barrier delivers all three reports in one invocation --------
    prompt skeptic(correctness, security, quality) -> ledger
    "
        Three reviewers running on three different models have just reported. Your
        job is to REFUTE them, not to agree with them.

        Correctness reviewer:
        $(correctness)

        Security reviewer:
        $(security)

        Design quality reviewer:
        $(quality)

        Take each claim and read the actual code to show that it is wrong: the
        guard already exists somewhere the reviewer did not look, that input cannot
        reach that path, the behaviour is deliberate, the API is being used
        correctly, the case is already covered by a test, or the finding is taste
        rather than a defect. Default to refuted when you are not sure. A claim
        survives only if you can state the concrete failure it causes. A finding
        that two reviewers reported independently still has to survive on the
        evidence and not on the vote count. Do not modify anything.

        Set `ledger` to exactly this shape:
          CONFIRMED:
          <numbered surviving findings, each with file, defect, failure scenario,
           and severity high, medium or low. Write `none` if nothing survived.>
          REFUTED:
          <numbered refuted claims, one line each with the reason it does not hold.
           Write `none` if nothing was refuted.>
          NEW THIS ROUND: <how many findings you just added to CONFIRMED>
          UNEXAMINED: <files or concerns in the diff that no reviewer touched this
           round, or `none`>
    "

    // ---- merge, then loop or finish ------------------------------------------
    prompt coordinator(ledger) -> (round_brief | report)
    "
        The skeptic has returned this round's verdicts:
        $(ledger)

        You wrote the previous briefing, so you already know the PR number, the
        round number, the round cap, and everything confirmed or refuted in earlier
        rounds. Merge this round's verdicts into those running lists.

        Set exactly one effect.

        Set `round_brief` for another round when this round added at least one new
        confirmed finding AND the round number is still below the cap. It repeats
        the `PR:` line, gives `ROUND: <n> of <cap>` with the number incremented,
        restates the merged `CONFIRMED SO FAR:` and `REFUTED SO FAR:` lists in full
        so the reviewers do not re-report them, and points the reviewers at what the
        skeptic listed as unexamined. Name those files and concerns explicitly.
        Keep it under 400 words.

        Otherwise set `report`, because the review has converged: either this round
        added nothing new, or the cap has been reached. The report contains the PR
        number and title, a one-paragraph verdict recommending approve or request
        changes, the confirmed findings grouped by severity with file, defect and
        failure scenario, a short list of the most notable refuted claims so the
        author can see they were considered and dismissed, and the number of rounds
        that ran. Nothing has been posted to GitHub, so this text is the entire
        deliverable.
    "
}

main {
    review = AdversarialPRReview()
}
